      "text": "Solution: $A_1A_2A_4$ forms an equilateral triangle with vertices in the set as can be seen in the diagram above, and so do $A_1A_3A_6$ and $A_2A_3A_6$. Hence, we need to count the number of distinct triangles formed by three vertices in $\\{A_3, A_4, \\ldots, A_8, A_9\\}$. Among these triangles, $A_7A_8A_9$ forms an equilateral triangle with vertices in the set, and $A_7A_8A_9$, $A_8A_9A_1$, and $A_9A_1A_2$ also form equilateral triangles with vertices in the set. The rest of the triangles do not form equilateral triangles with vertices in the given set.\n\nThus, there are a total of $8+7+5+4+3+2+1=30$ distinct equilateral triangles that can be formed with the given vertices. Alternatively, since the equilateral triangles with at least two vertices in the set are more likely to be formed by 3 consecutive vertices, and there are 6 sets of 3 consecutive vertices, there are $9\\times 6 = 54$ possible combinations of vertices. Among these possibilities, $10+9+8+7+6+5+4 = 45$ combinations result in a tetrahedron (as their vertices alternate). This leaves $45-30=15$ triangles possible for which two or more vertices are consecutive. If a given vertex is not constrained by a vertex of the same set, then it can only be the symmetric vertex of another vertex in its set. If the vertex is constrained, there are $n$ possibilities for a vertex that can be adjacent, where $n$ is the cardinality of its set. For instance, if 2 out of 3 vertices are constrained to a set, there are $3-2 = 1$ possibility for the vertices that can be adjacent, and there are $n-1$ different sets for computing the cardinality by performing a symmetric difference with the set the vertex is restricted to. Thus, the remaining triangles are given by $$\\sum_{S} (n-1)\\times (n-2)! \\times n \\times 2$$, for all subsets of the unchanged sets. When applying the stars and bars method, the $n-1$ terms will appear when `n-1' comes from the expanding of $\\binom{n}{k}$ to compute the cardinality of a set of $k$ different elements. $n$ will come from the 2 different combinations from $k=n - 2$ (the new sets are chosen to retain the pure subsets of the given sets), $$\\sum_{S} (n-1)\\times (n-2)! \\times n$$ $\\implies 15$ is the final result. The value of $n$ is not constrained for this solution as a triangle can be formed with two vertices in the set and one vertex not in the set, and every such triangle will have such a form. By doing this for all combinations of the sets and including the combinations further, we find that the final value is $$2\\times (\\sum_{S} (n-1)\\times (n-2)! \\times n + \\sum_{S} (n-1)\\times (n-2)! \\times n \\times 2)$$, which simplifies to $\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)!by40\\boxed{15}$ triangles.",
